=== HINT 1: Get EtwEventWrite ===
BYTE *fn = (BYTE*)GetProcAddress(GetModuleHandleA("ntdll.dll"), "EtwEventWrite");

=== HINT 2: Unlock and patch (single ret byte) ===
DWORD old = 0;
VirtualProtect(fn, 1, PAGE_EXECUTE_READWRITE, &old);
*fn = 0xC3;
VirtualProtect(fn, 1, old, &old);

=== KEY INSIGHT ===
EtwEventWrite patch silences ALL user-mode ETW events from this process:
  PowerShell Script Block Logging, .NET CLR events, AMSI events, custom ETW providers.
Kernel ETW (via kernel-mode providers) is unaffected - cannot be patched from user mode.
Detection: CRC checks on ntdll.dll functions; integrity monitoring; Kernel Patch Guard (KPP).
More surgical: patch only specific providers
  (EtwEventEnabled, EtwEventRegister) to target PowerShell telemetry specifically.
Alternative: GUID-based provider disable via EnableTraceEx2 if you control the process.
Modern EDR hooks EtwEventWrite itself to detect patching - use direct syscall or
  map a clean ntdll (md13 technique) to get an unhooked copy to call.
